"[1710.09745] Pushing Memory Bandwidth Limitations Through Efficient Implementations of Block-Krylov Space Solvers on GPUs (https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.09745)\nciteturn3view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 6 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.09745\",\"lineno\":20}); Total lines: 168\nL16: > Abstract:Lattice quantum chromodynamics simulations in nuclear physics have benefited from a tremendous number of algorithmic advances such as multigrid and eigenvector deflation. These improve the time to solution but do not alleviate the intrinsic memory-bandwidth constraints of the matrix-vector operation dominating iterative solvers. Batching this operation for multiple vectors and exploiting cache and register blocking can yield a super-linear speed up.\nL17: Block-Krylov solvers can naturally take advantage of such batched matrix-vector operations, further reducing the iterations to solution by sharing the Krylov space between solves. However, practical implementations typically suffer from the quadratic scaling in the number of vector-vector operations. Using the QUDA library, we present an implementation of a block-CG solver on NVIDIA GPUs which reduces the memory-bandwidth complexity of vector-vector operations from quadratic to linear.\nL18: We present results for the HISQ discretization, showing a 5x speedup compared to highly-optimized independent Krylov solves on NVIDIA's SaturnV cluster.\nL19: Comments:  | 15 pages, 14 figures, in press\nL20: Subjects:  | High Energy Physics - Lattice (hep-lat); Computational Physics (physics.comp-ph)\nL21: Report number:  | FERMILAB-PUB-17-592-CD\nL22: Cite as:  | 【13†arXiv:1710.09745】 [hep-lat]\nL23:    | (or 【14†arXiv:1710.09745v2】 [hep-lat] for this version)\nL24:    | 【15†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.1710.09745†doi.org】 arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL25: Journal reference:  | Comp. Phys. Comm. 2018\nL26: Related DOI (Digital Object Identifier):  | 【16†https://doi.org/10.1016/j.cpc.2018.06.019†doi.org】 DOI(s) linking to related resources\nL27: ## Submission history\nL28: \nL29: From: Alejandro Marino Vaquero Avilés-Casco [【17†view email】]\nL30: 【5†[v1]】 Thu, 26 Oct 2017 15:04:25 UTC (297 KB)\nL31: [v2] Tue, 7 Aug 2018 19:13:50 UTC (565 KB)\nL32: \nL33: Full-text links:\nL34: \nL35: ## Access Paper:\nL36: \nL37: View a PDF of the paper titled Pushing Memory Bandwidth Limitations Through Efficient Implementations of Block-Krylov Space Solvers on GPUs, by M. A. Clark and 3 other authors\nL38: \nL39:   * 【11†View PDF】\nL40:   * 【12†HTML (experimental)】\nL41:   * 【18†TeX Source】\nL42: \nL43: 【19†view license】\nL44: ### Current browse context:\nL45: \nL46: hep-lat\nL47: \nL48: 【20†< prev】   |   【21†next >】\nL49: \nL50: 【22†new】 | 【23†recent】 | 【24†2017-10】\nL51: \nL52: Change to browse by:\nL53: \nL54: 【25†physics】\nL55: 【26†physics.comp-ph】\nL56: \nL57: ### References & Citations\nL58: \nL59:   * 【27†INSPIRE HEP†inspirehep.net】\nL60:   * 【28†SciX†scixplorer.org】\nL61:   * 【29†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com】\nL62:   * 【30†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org】\nL63: \nL64: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL65: \nL66: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL67: \nL68: [Button: ×]\nL69: \nL70: loading...\nL71: \nL72: Data provided by:\nL73: ### Bookmark\nL74: \nL75: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL76: \nL77: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL78: \nL79: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL80: \nL81: Bibliographic Explorer (【31†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org】)\nL82: \nL83: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL84: \nL85: Connected Papers (【32†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com】)\nL86: \nL87: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL88: \nL89: Litmaps (【33†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co】)\nL90: \nL91: [Input] scite.ai Toggle\nL92: \nL93: scite Smart Citations (【34†What are Smart Citations?†www.scite.ai】)\nL94: \nL95: [Input] Code, Data, Media\nL96: # Code, Data and Media Associated with this Article\nL97: \nL98: [Input] alphaXiv Toggle\nL99: \nL100: alphaXiv (【35†What is alphaXiv?†alphaxiv.org】)\nL101: \nL102: [Input] Links to Code Toggle\nL103: \nL104: CatalyzeX Code Finder for Papers (【36†What is CatalyzeX?†www.catalyzex.com】)\nL105: \nL106: [Input] DagsHub Toggle\nL107: \nL108: DagsHub (【37†What is DagsHub?†dagshub.com】)\nL109: \nL110: [Input] GotitPub Toggle\nL111: \nL112: Gotit.pub (【38†What is GotitPub?†gotit.pub】)\nL113: \nL114: [Input] Huggingface Toggle\nL115: \nL116: Hugging Face (【39†What is Huggingface?†huggingface.co】)\nL117: \nL118: [Input] ScienceCast Toggle\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn3view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/snarang181/zk-duel/commits/master/\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://github.com/snarang181/zk-duel/commits/master/: Cache miss--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn3view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://github.com/snarang181/zk-duel/blob/master/LICENSE\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://github.com/snarang181/zk-duel/blob/master/LICENSE: Cache miss--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n1. NVIDIA Blackwell Tuning Guide — Blackwell Tuning Guide 13.4 documentation (https://docs.nvidia.com/cuda/blackwell-tuning-guide/index.html)\nciteturn3view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view2\",\"pattern\":\"register\"}); Total lines: 121\nL22:   * Adjust kernel launch configuration to maximize device utilization.\nL23: \nL24:   * Ensure that global memory accesses are coalesced.\nL25: \nL26:   * Minimize redundant accesses to global memory whenever possible.\nL27: \nL28:   * Avoid long sequences of diverged execution by threads within the same warp.\nL29: ## 1.3. Application Compatibility【7†】\nL30: \nL31: Before addressing specific performance tuning issues covered in this guide, refer to the 【8†Blackwell Compatibility Guide for CUDA Applications】 to ensure that your application is compiled in a way that is compatible with NVIDIA Blackwell.\nL32: \nL33: ## 1.4. NVIDIA Blackwell Tuning【9†】\nL34: \nL35: ### 1.4.1. Streaming Multiprocessor【10†】\nL36: \nL37: The NVIDIA Blackwell Streaming Multiprocessor (SM) provides the following improvements over the NVIDIA Hopper GPU architecture.\nL38: #### 1.4.1.1. Occupancy【11†】\nL39: \nL40: The maximum number of concurrent warps per SM is 64 for compute capability 10.0 and 48 for compute capability 12.0. Other 【12†factors influencing warp occupancy】 are:\nL41: \nL42:   * The register file size is 64K 32-bit registers per SM.\nL43: \nL44:   * The maximum number of registers per thread is 255.\nL45: \nL46:   * The maximum number of thread blocks per SM is 32 for devices of compute capability 10.0 and 12.0.\nL47:   * For devices of compute capability 10.0 shared memory capacity per SM is 228 KB. For devices of compute capability 12.0, shared memory capacity per SM is 128KB.\nL48: \nL49:   * For devices of compute capability 10.0 the maximum shared memory per thread block is 227 KB. For devices of compute capability 12.0 the maximum shared memory per thread block is 99 KB.\nL50:   * For applications using Thread Block Clusters, it is always recommended to compute the occupancy using `cudaOccupancyMaxActiveClusters` and launch cluster-based kernels accordingly.\nL51: \nL52: Overall, developers can expect similar occupancy as on NVIDIA Hopper GPU architecture GPUs without changes to their application.\nL53: #### 1.4.1.2. Thread Block Clusters【13†】\nL54: NVIDIA Hopper Architecture added a new optional level of hierarchy, Thread Block Clusters, that allows for further possibilities when parallelizing applications. Thread block clusters are supported by Blackwell GPUs as well. A thread block can read from, write to, and perform atomics in shared memory of other thread blocks within its cluster. This is known as Distributed Shared Memory.\nL55: As demonstrated in the 【14†CUDA Programming Guide】, there are applications that cannot fit required data within shared memory and must use global memory instead. Distributed shared memory can act as an intermediate step between these two options.\nL56: Distributed Shared Memory can be used by an SM simultaneously with L2 cache accesses. This can benefit applications that need to communicate data between SMs by utilizing the combined bandwidth of both distributed shared memory and L2.\nL57: In order to achieve best performance for accesses to Distributed Shared Memory, access patterns to those described in the 【15†CUDA C++ Best Practices Guide for Global Memory】 should be used. Specifically, accesses to Distributed Shared Memory should be coalesced and aligned to 32-byte segments, if possible.\nL58: Access patterns with non-unit stride should be avoided if possible, which can be achieved by using local shared memory, similar to what is shown in the 【16†CUDA C++ Best Practices Guide for Shared Memory】.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - snarang181/zk-duel · GitHub (https://github.com/snarang181/zk-duel)\nciteturn3view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view1\",\"pattern\":\"register\"}); Total lines: 438\nL344: The A100 results are an existing dataset and are not rerun by the script. On an A100, `experiments/a100/followups/run_a100_followups.sh` reruns the kernel-level follow-ups (`experiments/a100/followups/README.md`). The individual scripts behind every recorded dataset are listed in `experiments/README.md`. More detail: `docs/reproducing.md`.\nL345: ## Where the reported data lives\nL346: \nL347: Paths are relative to `experiments/`. The analysis is in `h100/FINDINGS.md`; its tables T1–T19 are in `h100/FINDINGS_TABLES.md`, generated from the files below by `h100/tools/make_findings_tables.py`.\nL348: Result  | Data  | T-tables\nL349: --- | --- | ---\nL350: H100 zkPHIRE tier by width and by family (paper Tables 2 and 4)  | `h100/sweep_fixed/zk_32_128.csv`, `h100/sweep_fixed/zk_256.csv`  | none; summarize with `summarize_sweep.py` (below)\nL351: H100 diagnostic tier (paper Figure 4)  | `h100/sweep_fixed/diag_tier.csv`  | none\nL352: Wall-clock vs GPU-kernel time, and the `tl.reduce` ablation (paper Table 3)  | `h100/sweep_fixed/baseline_fixed_all.jsonl` (32-bit rows from `h100/sweep_fixed/baseline_fixed_32.jsonl`, a rerun on a quiet host), `h100/diag/treduce_all.jsonl`  | T19, T5\nL353: The same split with the first harness  | `h100/diag/baseline_all.jsonl`  | T4, T13\nL354: Occupancy ablation  | `h100/diag/occupancy.jsonl`  | T6\nL355: Register anatomy, hardware counters  | `h100/diag/register_anatomy.jsonl`, `h100/diag/ncu/`  | T10, T11\nL356: Layout and interpolation ablation; CUDA block size  | `h100/diag/layout_points.jsonl`, `h100/diag/cuda_block.jsonl`  | T15, T16\nL357: Triton compile time (`TritonGPUCoalesce`)  | `h100/diag/compile_profile.jsonl`, `h100/diag/compile_passes.jsonl`, `h100/diag/coalesce_scaling.jsonl`, `h100/logs/warm_jellyfish.log`  | T12, T17\nL358: Run-to-run noise  | `h100/diag/pin_noise.jsonl`  | T18\nL359: CPU-reference validation; r=28 int64-index reruns  | `h100/validation/`, `h100/diag/r28_int64_fix.jsonl`  | T7\nL360: A100 full sweep (A100-SXM4-80GB; portability)  | `a100/triton_cuda_field_resilient_r14_28_new.csv`  | T1–T3, T14\nL361: A100 PCIe kernel-level follow-ups  | `a100/followups/diag/`, compared in `a100/followups/COMPARISON.md`  | none\nL362: First H100 sweep; H100 sweep with `tl.reduce`; small-table host vs GPU check  | `h100/sweep/`, `h100/sweep_treduce/`, `h100/diag/small_table_64_128.jsonl`  | T1–T3, T8, T9\nL363: The fixed-harness sweep (`h100/sweep_fixed/`, run by `h100/run_fixed_sweep.sh`) uses the settings of the first H100 sweep (`h100/sweep/`) plus two harness fixes: the int64-index fix in both backends, and CUDA whole-run timing by a synchronized host timer, as in Triton. It adds the diagnostic tier and does not rerun `zk_jellyfish_zerocheck_hp` at 256 bits. T1–T3 are computed from the first sweep. To tabulate the fixed-harness sweep with the same definitions:\nL364:     python experiments/h100/tools/summarize_sweep.py experiments/h100/sweep_fixed/zk_32_128.csv \\\nL365:       experiments/h100/sweep_fixed/zk_256.csv\nL366: A row is validated when `triton_status` and `cuda_status` are `ok` and `checksum_match` is `true`; the ratio is Triton median / CUDA median.\nL367: ## Correctness checks\nL368: \nL369:   * Cross-backend agreement. The comparison drivers record both checksums and `checksum_match`. They leave `cuda_speedup_vs_triton` blank for any row that is not validated, print `WARNING: CHECKSUM MISMATCH` for each mismatch, and the summary tools exclude such rows (`docs/interpreting_results.md`).\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - snarang181/zk-duel · GitHub (https://github.com/snarang181/zk-duel)\nciteturn3view5 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view1\",\"pattern\":\"paper\"}); Total lines: 438\nL199: ## Repository files navigation\nL200: \nL201:   *   * 【90†README】\nL202:   * 【90†MIT license】\nL203: \nL204: More items\nL205: \nL206: # zkDuel\nL207: \nL208: zkDuel is the artifact of the paper zkDuel: Characterizing SumCheck Prover Kernels across GPU Programming Models (HASP '26). It contains matched Triton and CUDA SumCheck prover kernels over prime fields, the drivers that time and check them, and the data behind the paper's results.\nL209: \nL210: Repository: 【62†https://github.com/snarang181/zk-duel】\nL247: ## Repository layout\nL248: \nL249:     `src/zkduel/                    Python package: fields and modulus guard (fields.py), canonical\nL250:                                    workload expressions (workloads.py), CPU big-integer SumCheck\nL251:                                    reference (reference.py), 256-bit Triton backend (triton_backend/)\nL252:     benchmarks/field_sweep/        Prime-field drivers used for the paper:\nL253:       triton_sweep.py              Triton SumCheck for all 32 workloads and 4 widths\nL254:       compare_triton_cuda.py       Triton vs CUDA, joined CSV with checksum agreement\nL255:       resilient_compare_triton_cuda.py  Same, one subprocess per case, with a per-case timeout\nL256:       workload_specs.py            Workload metadata and aliases\nL257:       kernels/                     Triton expressions: poly.py (diagnostic tier), zk.py (zkPHIRE)\nL258:     benchmarks/u256/               Earlier 256-bit-only drivers (diagnostic-tier polynomials)\nL259:     benchmarks/wraparound_sweep/   Limb-width ablation with arithmetic mod 2^k (not a prime field)\nL260:     cuda/src/                      CUDA sources; field_sumcheck.cu is the paper's CUDA backend\nL261:     cuda/build/                    nvcc build scripts\nL262:     cuda/check/                    CPU checkers for the CUDA executables\nL263:     scripts/                       setup_env.sh, run_paper_experiments.sh, plot_zkduel.py\nL264:     tests/                         pytest suite (field guard, 256-bit arithmetic, CPU reference)\nL265:     docs/                          Benchmarks, implementation, reproduction, reading the results\nL266:     experiments/                   Data behind the paper and the scripts that produced it\nL267:       README.md                    Index: every dataset, the question it answers, its script\nL268:       h100/                        H100 PCIe, the primary platform: sweeps, diagnostics, validation,\nL269:                                    tools/, patches/, env/, logs/, FINDINGS.md, FINDINGS_TABLES.md\nL270:       a100/                        A100: the full sweep CSV and the A100 PCIe follow-ups (followups/)\nL271:     `\nL272: ## Requirements\nL273: \nL274: The paper's software stack:\nL275: Component  | Version  | Used for\nL276: --- | --- | ---\nL277: Python  | 3.12  | drivers, Triton\nL278: PyTorch  | 2.12.0+cu130  | device tensors for the Triton backend\nL279: Triton  | 3.7.0  | Triton backend (its bundled `ptxas`)\nL280: nvcc  | 12.9  | CUDA executable\nL281: OpenSSL headers (`libssl-dev`)  | 3.0.2 on the H100 host  | SHA3 challenges and BIGNUM in the CUDA executable\nL282: NVIDIA driver  | supports CUDA 13  | PyTorch cu130 runtime\nL283: Nsight Systems (`nsys`)  |  | GPU-kernel time in the diagnostic phases\nL284: Nsight Compute (`ncu`)  |  | hardware counters only; needs root where profiling is restricted\nL285:   * GPU. The recorded results use an NVIDIA H100 PCIe 80 GB (sm_90) and A100 80 GB GPUs (sm_80). Configurations whose tables do not fit in 80 GB are skipped as out of memory.\nL286:   * Driver. If the kernel driver is older than CUDA 13 requires, NVIDIA's forward-compatibility user-mode driver (`cuda-compat`, datacenter GPUs) works. The paper's H100 host ran kernel module 570.148.08 with the 580.126.20 user-mode driver, loaded through `LD_LIBRARY_PATH` and `TRITON_LIBCUDA_PATH`. `experiments/h100/env_table5.sh` sets this up; its paths are those of the paper's host and need editing elsewhere.\nL287:   * Recorded platforms. `experiments/h100/env/table5_h100.md` and `experiments/h100/sweep_fixed/env/table5_h100_fixed.md` (H100), `experiments/a100/followups/env/table5_a100.md` (A100 PCIe). Each directory also holds `pip-freeze.txt`, `nvidia-smi-q.txt` and `lscpu.txt`.\nL288: ## Quick start\nL289: \nL290: Set up the environment. `scripts/setup_env.sh` creates a Python environment with the paper's stack and checks for `nvcc` and the OpenSSL headers; activate that environment before the commands below. By hand, the equivalent is roughly:\nL291: \nL292:     python3.12 -m venv .venv && . .venv/bin/activate\nL293:     pip install torch==2.12.0 --index-url https://download.pytorch.org/whl/cu130\nL294:     pip install triton==3.7.0\nL295:     pip install -e '.[dev,plot]'\nL296: Then, from the repository root:\nL297: \nL298:     export PYTHONPATH=src\nL299:     mkdir -p results/quick\nL300: \nL301:     # Unit tests. The field-guard tests need no GPU; the Triton tests do.\nL302:     python -m pytest -q\nL303: \nL304:     # Triton kernels of one workload against the CPU reference (r=3 and r=10).\nL305:     python -u experiments/h100/tools/validate_vs_registry.py --bit-width 32 \\\nL306:       --point-mode specialized --workloads zk_spartan_2 --out results/quick/validate.jsonl\nL307: \nL308:     # Triton timing for one workload at a small size.\nL309:     python -u benchmarks/field_sweep/triton_sweep.py --workloads zk_spartan_2 \\\nL310:       --bit-widths 32 --rounds 12 --warmups 1 --repeats 5 --point-mode specialized \\\nL311:       --out results/quick/triton.csv\nL312: These take minutes, most of it Triton compilation. For a Triton-vs-CUDA comparison, build the CUDA executable once and run the joined driver:\nL313: \nL314:     cuda/build/build_field_sumcheck.sh        # about 1 h: every workload at every width\nL315:     python -u benchmarks/field_sweep/compare_triton_cuda.py --skip-build \\\nL316:       --workloads zk_spartan_2 --bit-widths 32 --rounds 12 --warmups 1 --repeats 5 \\\nL317:       --point-mode specialized --out results/quick/compare.csv\nL318: `checksum_match` must be `true`; `cuda_speedup_vs_triton` is Triton time / CUDA time. `CUDA_ARCH` overrides the default `-arch=native`. If the OpenSSL headers are not in the default include path, use `experiments/h100/build_field_sumcheck.sh` with `ZKDUEL_OPENSSL_INCLUDE` set. `results/` is git-ignored.\nL319: ## Reproducing the paper\nL320: \nL321:     scripts/setup_env.sh\nL322:     scripts/run_paper_experiments.sh [--dry-run] [--phases \"LIST\"] [--out DIR] [--skip-gate] \\\nL323:       [--jobs N] [--include-jellyfish256] [--force]\nL324:   * Output goes to `results/paper_run` (git-ignored) unless `--out DIR` is given.\nL325:   * `--dry-run` shows what would run. `--phases \"LIST\"` runs only the listed phases.\nL326:   * Timing-sensitive phases are preceded by a host-latency gate; `--skip-gate` skips it. The gate exists because the paper's host showed launch and synchronization latency drifting during the day while GPU-kernel time did not (`experiments/h100/sweep_fixed/probe.log`).\nL327:   * `zk_jellyfish_zerocheck_hp` at 256 bits is skipped unless `--include-jellyfish256`: its Triton compile takes hours.\nL328:   * See `scripts/run_paper_experiments.sh --help` for `--jobs` and `--force`.\nL329: Phases, in order. Durations are approximate, on one H100; a full run is on the order of a working day.\nL330: Phase  | Content  | Recorded counterpart (under `experiments/h100/`)  | Time\nL331: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL332: `env`  | environment record  | `sweep_fixed/env/`  |\nL333: `build`  | CUDA executable  |  | ~1 h\nL334: `validate`  | both backends against the CPU reference  | `validation/`  |\nL335: `precompile`  | Triton kernels into the cache before any timing (CPU)  |  | ~1–2 h\nL336: `sweep`  | zkPHIRE tier and diagnostic tier  | `sweep_fixed/`  | ~1.7 h\nL337: `split`  | wall-clock vs GPU-kernel time (Nsight Systems)  | `sweep_fixed/baseline_fixed_all.jsonl`, `sweep_fixed/baseline_fixed_32.jsonl`, `diag/treduce_all.jsonl`  | ~1 h\nL338: `occupancy`  | CUDA occupancy ablation  | `diag/occupancy.jsonl`  |\nL339: `ablations`  | layout x interpolation, CUDA block size  | `diag/layout_points.jsonl`, `diag/cuda_block.jsonl`  | ~1.5 h\nL340: `counters`  | Nsight Compute counters (needs `sudo`)  | `diag/ncu/`  |\nL341: `compile`  | Triton compile-time profile  | `diag/compile_profile.jsonl`, `diag/compile_passes.jsonl`, `diag/coalesce_scaling.jsonl`  | ~1 h\nL342: `noise`  | run-to-run noise and vCPU pinning  | `diag/pin_noise.jsonl`  |\nL343: `tables`  | summary tables of the run  |  |\nL344: The A100 results are an existing dataset and are not rerun by the script. On an A100, `experiments/a100/followups/run_a100_followups.sh` reruns the kernel-level follow-ups (`experiments/a100/followups/README.md`). The individual scripts behind every recorded dataset are listed in `experiments/README.md`. More detail: `docs/reproducing.md`.\nL345: ## Where the reported data lives\nL346: \nL347: Paths are relative to `experiments/`. The analysis is in `h100/FINDINGS.md`; its tables T1–T19 are in `h100/FINDINGS_TABLES.md`, generated from the files below by `h100/tools/make_findings_tables.py`.\nL348: Result  | Data  | T-tables\nL349: --- | --- | ---\nL350: H100 zkPHIRE tier by width and by family (paper Tables 2 and 4)  | `h100/sweep_fixed/zk_32_128.csv`, `h100/sweep_fixed/zk_256.csv`  | none; summarize with `summarize_sweep.py` (below)\nL351: H100 diagnostic tier (paper Figure 4)  | `h100/sweep_fixed/diag_tier.csv`  | none\nL352: Wall-clock vs GPU-kernel time, and the `tl.reduce` ablation (paper Table 3)  | `h100/sweep_fixed/baseline_fixed_all.jsonl` (32-bit rows from `h100/sweep_fixed/baseline_fixed_32.jsonl`, a rerun on a quiet host), `h100/diag/treduce_all.jsonl`  | T19, T5\nL353: The same split with the first harness  | `h100/diag/baseline_all.jsonl`  | T4, T13\nL354: Occupancy ablation  | `h100/diag/occupancy.jsonl`  | T6\nL355: Register anatomy, hardware counters  | `h100/diag/register_anatomy.jsonl`, `h100/diag/ncu/`  | T10, T11\nL356: Layout and interpolation ablation; CUDA block size  | `h100/diag/layout_points.jsonl`, `h100/diag/cuda_block.jsonl`  | T15, T16\nL357: Triton compile time (`TritonGPUCoalesce`)  | `h100/diag/compile_profile.jsonl`, `h100/diag/compile_passes.jsonl`, `h100/diag/coalesce_scaling.jsonl`, `h100/logs/warm_jellyfish.log`  | T12, T17\nL358: Run-to-run noise  | `h100/diag/pin_noise.jsonl`  | T18\nL359: CPU-reference validation; r=28 int64-index reruns  | `h100/validation/`, `h100/diag/r28_int64_fix.jsonl`  | T7\nL360: A100 full sweep (A100-SXM4-80GB; portability)  | `a100/triton_cuda_field_resilient_r14_28_new.csv`  | T1–T3, T14\nL361: A100 PCIe kernel-level follow-ups  | `a100/followups/diag/`, compared in `a100/followups/COMPARISON.md`  | none\nL370:   * CPU reference. `experiments/h100/tools/validate_vs_registry.py` checks the Triton kernels against the canonical expressions with plain modular arithmetic, for all 32 workloads, by default at r=3 and r=10 with fixed challenges and at r=10 with SHA3 challenges. `experiments/h100/tools/validate_cuda_vs_registry.py` checks the CUDA executable against the same reference checksums (the paper's CUDA binary: all 381 cases, plus jellyfish zerocheck at 256 bits).\nL378:   * Compiles and timing. Warm the Triton cache before timed runs (the `precompile` phase). A compile running concurrently with a timed run distorts it.\nL379:   * Host paths. `experiments/h100/env_table5.sh` and several scripts under `experiments/` contain absolute paths of the paper's hosts.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nGitHub - SPIRE-GMU/sum_cats · GitHub (https://github.com/SPIRE-GMU/sum_cats)\nciteturn3view6 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn1view0\",\"pattern\":\"LICENSE\"}); Total lines: 235\nNo matching text found for \"LICENSE\"--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nBuilding Faster Cryptography with Carryless Multiplication in NVIDIA CUDA 13.3 | NVIDIA Technical Blog (https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/building-faster-cryptography-with-carryless-multiplication-in-nvidia-cuda-13-3/)\nciteturn3view7 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: find({\"ref_id\":\"turn2view0\",\"pattern\":\"2026\"}); Total lines: 146\nL0: 【0†DEVELOPER】\nL1: \nL2: 【1†Technical Blog】\nL3: \nL4: [Input: Search blog] [Input] [Input]\nL5: \nL6: 【2†Trustworthy AI / Cybersecurity】\nL7: \nL8: [Select]\nL9: \nL10: # Building Faster Cryptography with Carryless Multiplication in NVIDIA CUDA 13.3\nL11: \nL12: 【3†Image: Decorative image.†developer-blogs.nvidia.com】\nL13: \nL14: Jul 15, 2026\nL15: \nL16: By 【4†Jean-Luc Watson】, 【5†Charles Gouert】, 【6†Michael B. Sullivan】 and 【7†G. Edward Suh】\nL17: \nL18: [Button: Like ]\nL19: \nL20: 【8†Discuss (0)】\nL21: ## AI-Generated Summary\nL22:   * NVIDIA CUDA 13.3 introduces clmad, a hardware-accelerated PTX instruction for carryless multiply-accumulate on all Ampere and newer GPUs (SM 80+).\nL23:   * GHASH throughput reaches ~6.3 TB/s on the NVIDIA B200, up to 18.8x faster than the prior bitsliced state of the art and close to DRAM read bandwidth.\nL24:   * Sum-check protocol performance over GF(2^128) improves by 4–13x versus the previous bitsliced approach, with gains scaling alongside polynomial and composition sizes.\nL25:   * Carryless multiplication underpins a wide range of cryptographic and coding workloads including CRC, Reed–Solomon, BCH codes, quantum stabilizer codes, post-quantum schemes, and binary-field zero-knowledge systems such as Binius.\nL26: ### Next Steps\nL27: \nL28:   * Download 【9†CUDA 13.3】 to access the clmad instruction.\nL29:   * Explore the 【10†cuPQC SDK】 for post-quantum cryptographic workloads.\nL30:   * Review the 【11†NVIDIA Aerial documentation】 for telecom applications using carryless multiplication.\nL31: \nL32: Powered by NVIDIA Nemotron. AI-generated content may summarize information incompletely. Verify important information. 【12†Learn more†www.nvidia.com】\nL33: For over fifteen years, x86 CPUs have shipped with a dedicated hardware instruction for carryless multiplication. It’s a small but stubborn primitive that sits underneath authenticated encryption, error-correcting codes, and modern zero-knowledge proofs.\nL34: Until now, NVIDIA GPUs lacked native support for this operation. NVIDIA CUDA 13.3 closes that gap with `clmad`, a new 【13†PTX instruction†docs.nvidia.com】 available on all NVIDIA Ampere and newer GPUs (SM 80+). In this post, we show what is possible when carryless multiplication is finally a hardware-accelerated GPU primitive.\nL35: We benchmark two cryptographic workloads that depend on it: GHASH, the integrity hash inside AES-GCM (the AEAD cipher behind TLS, VPN, and most data-center encryption), and the sum-check protocol, the workhorse inner loop of advanced zero-knowledge proving systems. On the NVIDIA B200, GHASH throughput reaches ~6.3 TB/s—close to DRAM read bandwidth and up to 18.8x faster than the prior bitsliced state of the art. Sum-check over \\(GF(2^{128})\\) speeds up the prior state of the art by 4–13×.\n"
